%[text] # Working with Git and MATLAB
%[text] This workshop introduces two practical ways to use Git while working in MATLAB: using Git commands in the Command Window and using MATLAB's graphical tools. Both approaches work with the same repository and the same history.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Contents"}
%[text] - Before you begin
%[text] - Git concepts in one minute
%[text] - Hands-on Git workflow
%[text] - Intermediate users: file browser and projects
%[text] - A toolbox-ready project layout
%[text] - A dependable daily workflow
%[text] - Collaboration habits \
%%
%[text] ## Before you begin
%[text] Git records the evolution of files in a repository. It works locally: creating a commit does not send anything to a server. A remote such as GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, or an internal Git server is where you share commits with other people.
%[text] For this workshop, open MATLAB in the repository root: the folder containing the hidden `.git` directory.
%[text] MATLAB includes a Git API, so you do not need an external Git installation to work with a repository from MATLAB. Create a repository object with:
repo = gitrepo %[output:6888e3c3]
%[text] `repo` is a `matlab.git.GitRepository` object. Its methods let you inspect status and history, create and switch branches, commit, merge, pull, and push.
%[text] Alternatively, if Git is installed on your computer, you can call its command-line interface from the MATLAB Command Window. Confirm that MATLAB can find the external Git executable:
!git --version %[output:39c3495d]
%[text] For repeated command-line use, define this local helper once. It removes the leading `!`, so `git status` runs the same command as `!git status`:
function git(varargin)
cmd = sprintf("git %s",strjoin(varargin," "));
system(cmd);
end
%%
%[text] ## Git concepts in one minute
%[text] A file moves through a small number of Git states. You edit a file in your working folder, select the changes for the next commit by staging them, and then create a commit: a named, permanent snapshot in the local repository. A branch is a named line of work that lets you make a focused change without changing the main line of development.
%[text] The command `git status` is the most useful Git command. Run it often; it tells you which branch you are on, whether your files have changed, and what is staged.
%%
%[text] ## Hands-on Git workflow
%[text] Start on the `develop` branch and inspect the repository:
switchBranch(repo,"develop",Create=true) %[output:35b178bc]
statusDetails = status(repo) %[output:9ccce98e]
statusDetails.Status %[output:74c03695]
commitHistory = log(repo) %[output:90c8323a]
%[text] `status` reports the current state; `log` shows the commit history.
%%
%[text] ### Create and review a file
%[text] Create a small script, then open it in the Editor:
fid = fopen('myScript.m', 'w+');
fprintf(fid, 'numLags = 5;');
fclose(fid);
edit 'myScript.m'
%%
%[text] Inspect the new, untracked file:
statusDetails = status(repo);
statusDetails.Status %[output:5b4064b6]
%%
%[text] Stage the file, then inspect what the first commit will contain:
add(repo,"myScript.m")
statusDetails = status(repo); %[output:4e75827e]
statusDetails.Status %[output:27ed386c]
%%
commit(repo, "Files","myScript.m","Message","Added myScript.m") %[output:57e6681b]
statusDetail = status(repo);
statusDetail.Status %[output:7cef3822]
%%
%[text] Try now to make an edit to the file:
edit myScript.m
%%
%[text] Inspect the changes
git diff -- myScript.m %[output:9d9b61c6]
%[text] You can also compare the file graphically with **Source Control \> View Changes**:
%[text] ![](text:image:5a2c)
%%
%[text] Commit the modified version:
add(repo, "myScript.m")
commit(repo,Message="Changed to 4 lags",Files="myScript.m") %[output:92d4fa0d]
%%
%[text] ### Create, merge, and delete a focused branch
%[text] A branch is an independent line of development from a shared codebase; it lets you build and test a focused change safely, then review and merge it into develop when it is ready.
%[text] Use branches to isolate a focused change, so you can develop, review, and merge it without disrupting the shared develop branch.
%[text] Create a descriptive branch for the change:
switchBranch(repo, "change-num-lags", Create = true) %[output:9649afe2]
%%
git branch %[output:4fc8f37e]
%%
%[text] **Note:** ***We now switch to command-line Git for convenience and to show a second way of working with the same repository.***
%[text] Change the script, then stage and commit the focused change:
writelines("numLags = 5;","myScript.m")
git add myScript.m
git commit -m "Set number of lags to five" %[output:35941602]
git status %[output:81a86a1e]
%%
%[text] Return to `develop`, merge the completed change, and remove the branch:
git switch develop %[output:5e87465d]
git merge change-num-lags %[output:05539168]
%%
%[text] Alternatively, this workflow can be done programmatically via the branch manager:![](text:image:240e)
%%
git branch -d change-num-lags %[output:575d10fb]
%[text] Stage individual files rather than using `git add .` by habit. It makes each commit easier to review, understand, and revert.
%%
%[text] ### Share work through a Git server
%[text] A remote Git server such as GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, or an internal Git server is where your local repository meets the rest of the team. Use `pull` before starting work to retrieve compatible remote changes, and use `push` after committing to publish your local commits:
%[text] This diagram shows a shared Git server synchronizing the repositories of several MATLAB users. Each person pushes local commits to the server and pulls colleagues' published commits from it.
%[text] ![](text:image:9175)
%[text] `git pull`
%[text] `git push origin develop`
%%
%[text] ## Why MATLAB projects are recommended
%[text] A MATLAB project packages a repository with its working context: files, paths, shortcuts, project-specific settings, and source-control integration. It gives every collaborator the same convenient entry point.
%[text] To create one, select **New \> Project \> From Folder** in MATLAB and choose the repository root. If the folder already contains a Git repository, MATLAB detects it. Save the project file in the repository so teammates can open the same project.
%[text] Opening the project later restores its project path instead of relying on manually changed global MATLAB paths. Use the Project toolstrip to view files and source-control status, define startup shortcuts, and keep supporting code organised.
%[text] Do not add generated outputs, large data downloads, local credentials, or personal preference files to the repository. Add appropriate patterns to `.gitignore` before those files are accidentally staged.
%%
%[text] ## A toolbox-ready project layout
%[text] My preferred approach is to make the Git repository the MATLAB project and to keep the distributable toolbox, tests, and documentation in separate top-level folders. That structure keeps development, packaging, and continuous integration aligned from the first commit.
%[text] For example, a toolbox called SVAR could have the following layout:
%[text:table]
%[text] | Path | Purpose |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | `tbx/SVAR/` | Public toolbox code and **`Contents.m`** |
%[text] | `tbx/SVAR/+somefolder/` | Namespaced implementation that is not on the main path |
%[text] | `tests/` | `matlab.unittest` tests that validate toolbox behaviour |
%[text] | `doc/` | Getting-started material, examples, and release notes |
%[text] | `resources/` | Project metadata and package resources, if needed |
%[text] | `SVAR.prj` | The shared MATLAB project definition |
%[text] | `buildfile.m` and `.gitlab-ci.yml` | Repeatable local and CI checks, tests, and packaging |
%[text:table]
%[text] This separation has practical advantages:
%[text] - The toolbox package can include `tbx/SVAR` and selected documentation without shipping test fixtures, build outputs, or CI configuration.
%[text] - Tests can be run locally and in GitLab from the same `tests` folder, giving the team a repeatable quality gate.
%[text] - The project adds only the toolbox source folder to the MATLAB path; test helpers and repository tooling do not accidentally shadow user code.
%[text] - `Contents.m` provides a discoverable overview when users type `help SVAR` or browse the installed toolbox.
%[text] - `buildfile.m` makes package creation reproducible instead of depending on a sequence of clicks. \
%%
%[text] ### Create the layout programmatically
%[text] The following commands create a complete starter layout. Replace `svar` and the starter text with your toolbox name and description, then run this section in a new repository.
toolboxName = "svar";
toolboxFolder = fullfile("tbx",toolboxName);
mkdir(toolboxFolder)
mkdir("tests")
mkdir("doc")
contentsFile = fullfile(toolboxFolder,"Contents.m");
contentsLines = [
    "% SVAR - Structural Vector Autoregression Toolbox"
    "% Version 1.0.0 21-Sep-2026"
    "%"
    "% Functions"
    "%   svar     - Estimate and analyse structural vector autoregression models."
    "%"
    "% See also VARM"
    ];
writelines(contentsLines,contentsFile)
project = matlab.project.createProject(Folder=pwd,Name=toolboxName);
addFolderIncludingChildFiles(project,fullfile(pwd,"tbx")); %[output:3fb6fa01]
addFolderIncludingChildFiles(project,fullfile(pwd,"tests")); %[output:2702b4e6]
addFolderIncludingChildFiles(project,fullfile(pwd,"doc")); %[output:43729fab]
addPath(project,fullfile(pwd,"tbx",toolboxName)); %[output:9d1f31bc]
%%
%[text] ### Describe the toolbox with `Contents.m`
%[text] The previous code creates `tbx/svar/Contents.m` with `writelines`. Its first line is the toolbox H1 description; its version line lets MATLAB identify the toolbox. The generated starter file includes a description, version 1.0.0, date, and a list of public functions.
%[text] After the toolbox source folder is on the project path, use these commands to confirm that MATLAB can discover the toolbox:
v = ver('svar')
help svar
%[text] `ver('svar')` returns a structure with the name, version, and date read from `Contents.m`. `help svar` displays the same contents file as the toolbox landing page. Maintain this file as part of each release: update the version and date, and keep the public-function list accurate.
%[text] Add a `buildfile.m` at the repository root so local users and GitLab can both run `buildtool test` before `buildtool package`.
%[text] The package step can use `matlab.addons.toolbox.packageToolbox` with the project file or a `matlab.addons.toolbox.ToolboxOptions` object. Keep the generated `.mltbx` file in a release or build-artifacts folder and exclude it from ordinary source commits.
%%
%[text] ### Use Git tools from the open project
%[text] Once the project is open at the repository root, MATLAB recognises the Git repository and makes source-control information available in the Project and Current Folder panes. This is the graphical workflow for people who do not want to type Git commands.
%[text] 1. Refresh source-control status from the Project toolstrip or the Project pane to see modified, added, and conflicted files.
%[text] 2. Select one or more changed files, then use the source-control context menu to compare the working copy with its committed version before staging or committing.
%[text] 3. Commit related source, test, documentation, and project-definition changes together. Push only after the relevant tests pass.
%[text] 4. Pull before beginning a change; if MATLAB reports a conflict, resolve the affected file, rerun the tests, and commit the resolution. \
%[text] The same project root is available to MATLAB's programmatic Git interface, so GUI users and code-based workflows share one history:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] repo = gitrepo(project.RootFolder);
%[text] status(repo)
%[text] log(repo)
%[text] ```
%[text] Use the Project pane for visual review and `gitrepo` when you want to inspect or automate repository state from MATLAB code.
%%
%[text] ### Create the layout with the Project toolstrip
%[text] 1. Select **New \> Project \> From Folder**, choose the repository root, and save `toolboxName.prj` at that root.
%[text] 2. In the Project toolstrip, add the folders `tbx`, `tests`, and `doc` to the project. Create the folders in the Project pane if they do not yet exist.
%[text] 3. In the Project pane, right-click `tbx/toolboxName` and add that folder to the project path. Do not add `tests` to the permanent project path.
%[text] 4. Create `Contents.m` under `tbx/toolboxName`, then use the Project pane to mark it and the source files as project files.
%[text] 5. Use the Project toolstrip's **Share** or source-control controls to commit the `.prj` file, source, tests, documentation, `buildfile.m`, and `.gitlab-ci.yml`. \
%[text] Screenshot cue 1: capture the **New Project** menu with **From Folder** selected. Screenshot cue 2: capture the Project pane showing `tbx`, `tests`, and `doc`, with only `tbx/toolboxName` marked as a project-path folder. These two images make the toolbar route and the desired structure immediately visible.
%%
%[text] ## A dependable daily workflow
%[text] Use this short loop regardless of whether you prefer commands or menus:
%[text] 1. Open the MATLAB project or set the Current Folder to the repository root.
%[text] 2. Check the active branch and working state; pull the latest compatible changes.
%[text] 3. Create or switch to a task-specific branch.
%[text] 4. Make a small change and run the relevant script, live script, or test.
%[text] 5. Review the diff, stage only related files, and commit with a meaningful message.
%[text] 6. Push the branch and open a review or pull request when your team's process calls for it. \
%[text] Small commits make reviews clearer and make it much easier to locate, reuse, or revert a change.
%%
%[text] ## Collaboration habits
%[text] Treat text-based MATLAB files such as `.m` files as the default for code and live scripts when they need to be version controlled. Git can compare them line by line. Binary `.mlx` live scripts and `.mat` files are less reviewable and may create harder-to-resolve conflicts.
%[text] Before changing a shared file, pull first and communicate with teammates when you expect to edit the same area. Resolve conflicts in the affected file, run the code again, and commit the resolution; a conflict is a request for a human decision, not an error to hide.
%[text] Keep secrets out of Git. Never commit passwords, API keys, tokens, or private data. When in doubt, pause before staging and inspect `git diff --staged`.
%[text] The essential habit is simple: check status, make a focused change, test it, review the diff, commit it locally, and share it intentionally.

